      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-314895",
      ":question/problem": "An environmental researcher is tracking the depth $d$, in meters, of a lake during a dry season. The following table shows the rate at which the depth is decreasing.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(weeks)}$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${d}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{(meters/week)}$ | $-0.3$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.8$ | $-1.0$ |\n\nAt $t=2,$ the lake depth was $12$ meters. Use the tangent line approximation of $d$ at time $t=2$ to estimate the depth of the lake at $t=2.4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nmeters."
